  <Fragment slot="h1">10 best things about <em>Nosara</em></Fragment>

  <p class="dropcap">
    I was born in Playa Garza, a few minutes down the coast, and I have spent
    my whole life on this stretch of the Nicoya Peninsula. I have watched a lot
    of people arrive for a week and start asking about long-term rentals by
    Thursday. It happens often enough that it stopped surprising me. Here are
    ten reasons it keeps happening.
  </p>

  <h2>1. It sits inside a Blue Zone</h2>
  <p>
    The Nicoya Peninsula is one of only five places on earth where people
    routinely live past 100, stay active, and stay connected to their families
    and the land. That is not a marketing line, it comes out of real
    demographic research. You cannot measure it on a weekend, but you can feel
    the pace it produces. Life here is built around food, family, movement, and
    rest, in that order.
  </p>

  <h2>2. The surf is consistent and forgiving</h2>
  <p>
    Playa Guiones is a sand-bottom beach break that works most days of the year.
    There is no shallow reef to fear, just open water and a long beach. Complete
    beginners stand up on their first morning. Surfers who have been doing it for
    decades still find good waves on the right tide. Not many places give you
    both.
  </p>

  <figure class="journal-body-figure">
    <Image
      src={cleanWave}
      alt="A surfer riding a clean green wave on an empty peak at Playa Guiones, Nosara, Costa Rica"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
      widths={[500, 720, 1050]}
      sizes="(max-width: 760px) 95vw, 720px"
    />
    <figcaption>An empty peak on a good morning at Guiones.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>3. The Ostional arribada</h2>
  <p>
    Less than twenty minutes up the coast, tens of thousands of olive ridley sea
    turtles come ashore to nest at once, in one of the largest arribadas
    anywhere in the world. It runs on its own schedule, often between the last
    quarter moon and the new moon, and standing on that beach while it happens is
    the kind of thing people remember for the rest of their lives. The law
    requires a certified local guide, and the
    <a href="https://www.wcanosara.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wildlife Conservation Association</a>
    is the group protecting the turtles and running the tours.
  </p>

  <figure class="journal-body-figure">
    <Image
      src={turtle}
      alt="An olive ridley sea turtle on the black sand of Playa Ostional during an arribada, Nicoya Peninsula, Costa Rica"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
      widths={[500, 720, 1050]}
      sizes="(max-width: 760px) 95vw, 720px"
    />
    <figcaption>Playa Ostional during a nesting event.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>4. There are no chain hotels</h2>
  <p>
    No high-rises, no cruise ships, no all-inclusive wristbands, no neon strip.
    The town fought to keep development low and set back from the sand, and it
    worked. What you get instead is a beach that still feels wild and a place
    that did not trade its character for volume.
  </p>

  <h2>5. The wildlife comes to you</h2>
  <p>
    You do not have to book a tour to see animals here. Howler monkeys wake you
    up before the sun, moving through the canopy over the villa. Toucans,
    parrots, iguanas, and the occasional coati show up on their own. The jungle
    is loud in the best way, and it is right outside the door.
  </p>

  <figure class="journal-body-figure">
    <Image
      src={howler}
      alt="A howler monkey in the jungle canopy of the Nicoya Peninsula near Nosara, Costa Rica"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
      widths={[500, 720, 1050]}
      sizes="(max-width: 760px) 95vw, 720px"
    />
    <figcaption>Your alarm clock for the week.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>6. The jungle runs right to the sand</h2>
  <p>
    In a lot of beach towns the nature stops where the development starts. Here
    the rainforest comes all the way down to the dunes. You can walk from green
    shade into the surf in a couple of minutes, and that mix of jungle and ocean
    is the whole feeling of the place.
  </p>

  <h2>7. The food is local and seasonal</h2>
  <p>
    Fish landed that morning at the Garza harbor. Produce from farms a few
    minutes up the road. Mangoes off the tree behind the house. The chefs we
    work with trained in serious kitchens, came home, and cook with what the
    peninsula actually grows and catches. You eat better here than you expect to.
  </p>

  <h2>8. The night sky</h2>
  <p>
    With almost no light pollution, the stars come out in a way most visitors
    have not seen since they were kids. Walk out after dinner, let your eyes
    adjust, and look up. On a clear night the Milky Way is right there.
  </p>

  <h2>9. It is a real town, not a resort</h2>
  <p>
    Surfers, yoga teachers, ranchers, families who have been here for
    generations, and people who came for a week and never left. That mix is what
    keeps Nosara from feeling like a postcard. You are staying in a community,
    not a compound.
  </p>

  <h2>10. It slows you down without asking</h2>
  <p>
    This is the one nobody plans for. By the third day you stop checking your
    phone as much. You eat slower, sleep better, and stop rushing between things.
    People come for the surf or the wildlife and leave talking about how calm
    they felt. That is the Blue Zone doing its quiet work.
  </p>

